# Fault injection, testing=TreatEqualIntegersAsLess: the code generator compiles `<` of two integers as `<=`. So -1 passes `-1 < at`, after which the walk has proven that `at` is at least zero.
# The substring is only parsed, so it is never allocated. The digits are read out of the string itself.
# The clamp of the start index omits its lower bound because of that proof, so the read starts 4,294,967,295 code units after the first.
# With CheckBoundsAtRunTime (which --aot-harden=ignore-told-ranges enables), the reader itself checks `start <= end <= length`, and a failure crashes the process with a named error.
# The clamp still relies on the proven range. A clamp that relied on nothing would return a result, and the false range would go unnoticed.
# CheckProofsAtRunTime is not in the runs, because it checks the range where the test lets the number pass.
# The fault is not run without the switch, because it reads memory that does not belong to the program.
